<section class="hero">
  <div class="container hero-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">ASR operational systems layer</p>
      <h1>Different requests in. One clear operational workflow behind them.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This public-safe proof asset shows how ASR can turn appointments, room bookings, group visits and recurring requests into structured admin work — with records, status handling, reporting and export-ready data behind the public website.
      </p>
      <div class="badge-row">
        <span>Flexible intake</span>
        <span>Shared admin queue</span>
        <span>Availability logic</span>
        <span>Export-ready data</span>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/book">Try the booking workflows</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/admin">Open shared admin</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-card brand-card">
      <img class="asr-card-logo" src="/assets/asr_assets/ASR_Logo_Transparent.webp" alt="ASR Web Services">
      <h2>One reusable operational layer</h2>
      <p>
        The public form can change to suit the work. The operational principle stays the same: capture the right context, make it visible to staff, then move it through a manageable workflow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-soft">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Booking & enquiry patterns</p>
      <h2>One system can start in several different ways</h2>
      <p>
        The demo no longer assumes every organisation works like an appointment-led service business. Choose the intake pattern that most closely resembles the work you need to organise.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="booking-type-grid compact-type-grid">
      <% bookingTypes.forEach((type) => { %>
        <article class="booking-type-card">
          <span class="booking-type-kicker"><%= type.shortLabel %></span>
          <h3><%= type.label %></h3>
          <p><%= type.summary %></p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/book?type=<%= encodeURIComponent(type.value) %>">Try this flow →</a>
        </article>
      <% }); %>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container workflow-grid">
    <article>
      <p class="eyebrow">The operational problem</p>
      <h2>A form submission is only useful if the organisation can handle what happens next.</h2>
      <p>
        The same underlying workflow can support very different public requests while keeping the internal process calm, visible and consistent.
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Different public flows become structured requests</li>
        <li>Venue and recurring requests can be checked for sample clashes</li>
        <li>Staff can review everything in one queue and update workflow state</li>
        <li>Records, invoices and reporting can sit downstream where appropriate</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/workflow">See how the pieces connect</a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="note-card workflow-visual-card">
      <img src="/assets/booking-flow.svg" alt="Operational workflow illustration">
      <h3>Public-safe proof, adaptable logic</h3>
      <p>
        Everything here is fictional demo data. The value is the pattern: intake rules can change around an organisation without forcing every client into the same off-the-shelf process.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-soft">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Operational Modules</p>
      <h2>Follow the work beyond the booking form</h2>
      <p>
        Booking is the live entry point, but the wider proof asset also shows records, financial visibility and reporting patterns that can sit behind the same website.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-grid module-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Booking & Enquiry Workflow</h3>
        <p>Four public intake patterns feeding one shared admin queue, with status handling, clash checks and CSV export.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/book">Try booking workflows</a>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h3>Records Module</h3>
        <p>Public-safe operational records showing how context can carry forward after the initial request.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/modules/records">View records</a>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h3>Invoices & Reporting</h3>
        <p>Financial states, summary metrics and export routes that demonstrate downstream operational visibility.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/modules/reports">View reports</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section contact-band">
  <div class="container contact-band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Purpose-built, not one-size-fits-all</p>
      <h2>The demo proves building blocks. Discovery determines the real rules.</h2>
      <p>
        Payments, deposits, notifications, approval rules, user roles and deeper integrations are intentionally not assumed here. A real implementation would be shaped around the organisation’s actual process.
      </p>
      <a class="btn" href="/book">Explore the intake flows</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://asrweb.ie/portfolio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Back to ASR portfolio</a>
    </div>
    <img src="/assets/asr_assets/ASR_logo+mountainsFramed.webp" alt="ASR Web Services mountain framed artwork">
  </div>
</section>
